WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.479
Exercises turn continuity plans into operational capability.

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:07.340
Tabletop exercises let teams practice decisions, roles, escalation,

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.754
and communications without affecting production.

00:00:09.754 --> 00:00:14.580
Scenarios should challenge assumptions such as unavailable staff, corrupted backups,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.476
failed communications, or delayed vendor support.

00:00:17.476 --> 00:00:21.821
Operational drills test specific actions: switching to alternate communications,

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:26.647
locating offline procedures, starting backup power, moving to degraded mode,

00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:29.061
or coordinating a controlled shutdown.

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:32.922
Scope drills carefully with safety and engineering approval.

00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:36.784
Recovery testing proves that backups, spares, procedures, credentials,

00:00:36.784 --> 00:00:38.714
and dependencies work together.

00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:43.541
Measure actual time, data loss, manual effort, and validation steps.

00:00:43.541 --> 00:00:45.955
Testing isolated components is useful,

00:00:45.955 --> 00:00:49.816
but end-to-end exercises reveal sequencing and ownership gaps.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:54.643
Assign a plan owner and owners for every recovery capability.

00:00:54.643 --> 00:00:58.987
Maintain contact lists, architecture diagrams, vendor information, spare inventories,

00:00:58.987 --> 00:01:01.883
software media, licenses, and recovery sites.

00:01:01.883 --> 00:01:06.227
Review after personnel, process, equipment, network, or supplier changes.

00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:10.089
Integrate safety, operations, engineering, maintenance, cybersecurity, IT, facilities,

00:01:10.089 --> 00:01:12.985
communications, legal, leadership, and relevant vendors.

00:01:12.985 --> 00:01:16.363
Each group sees different consequences and dependencies.

00:01:16.363 --> 00:01:20.225
Governance should resolve decision authority before an emergency.

00:01:20.225 --> 00:01:25.534
After every exercise and incident, record lessons, assign actions, fund improvements,

00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:26.500
and retest.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.844
Useful metrics include recovery success, achieved objectives, untested assets,

00:01:30.844 --> 00:01:34.705
stale documentation, unavailable contacts, and overdue corrective work.

00:01:34.705 --> 00:01:40.980
A continuity program remains credible only when it is practiced, maintained, and improved.
